{"records":[{"id":"xbx248k5ft","title":"DYRK1A-TGFβ Signaling Axis Determines Sensitivity to OXPHOS Inhibition in Hepatocellular Carcinoma","description":"Intervening in mitochondrial OXPHOS has emerged as a potential therapeutic strategy for certain types of cancers. Employing kinome-based CRISPR screen, we find that knockout of DYRK1A synergizes with OXPHOS inhibitor IACS-010759 in liver cancer cells. Targeting DYRK1A combined with OXPHOS inhibitors activates TGFβ signaling, which is crucial for OXPHOS inhibition-triggered cell death. Mechanistically, upregulation of glutamine transporter SLC1A5 transcription compensates for the increased glutamine requirement upon OXPHOS inhibition. DYRK1A directly phosphorylates SMAD3 Thr132, thereby suppressing the negative impact of TGFβ signaling on transcription of SLC1A5, leading to intrinsic resistance of liver cancer cells to OXPHOS inhibition. Moreover, we demonstrate the therapeutic efficacy of IACS-010759 in combination with DYRK1A inhibition in multiple liver cancer models, including xenograftes, patient-derived xenograftes and spontaneous tumor model. Our study elucidates how the DYRK1A-TGFβ signaling axis controls the response of tumor cells to OXPHOS inhibition and provides valuable insights into targeting OXPHOS for liver cancer therapy. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-13T15:22:10.239Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xbx248k5ft","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xbx248k5ft"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ying","last_name":"cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kpjwcp4cnp","title":"Data for: hENT1 Reverses Chemoresistance by Regulating Glycolysis in Pancreatic Cancer.","description":"These data include the original data of in vitro and in vivo experiments. \"P VS PR\" means: comparing results between GEM-resistant pancreatic cancer cells and their parental sensitive ones. \"TOTAL\" means: comparing results among GEM-resistant pancreatic cancer cells, parental sensitive pancreatic cancer cells and the two cells transfected with lentivirus carrying Lv-hENT1 plasmid. GEM- group and GEM+ group were set in all experiments. In vitro tests, GEM+ group means: cells treated with 33.3nmol/L GEM in medium for 24h. In vivo tests, GEM+ group means: BALB/c nude mice treated with 3.33nmol/kg GEM by oral every other day after tumorigenesis for 4 times.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-26T20:11:36.439Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kpjwcp4cnp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kpjwcp4cnp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Biao","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Survival Analysis","Dose Toxicity","Student's t-Test","Curve"],"journal_issn":["03043835"]},{"id":"y66rnsbftw","title":"Reprograming immunosuppressive microenvironment by eIF4G1 targeting to eradicate pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","description":"Raw-data of \"Reprograming immunosuppressive microenvironment by eIF4G1 targeting to eradicate pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-09T18:17:43.008Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y66rnsbftw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y66rnsbftw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Li-hong","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v475rd4282","title":"Prognostic Prediction and Investigation of Underlying Immune Mechanisms in Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma Based on Immunogenic Death-Associated lncRNA Expression：Dataset and microscopic images","description":"We accessed the GeneCards database and identified 177 genes associated with ICD, with a score greater than 35. Data from TCGA , including transcriptome expression profiles, single nucleotide mutation data, clinical characteristic data, and survival information for patients diagnosed with pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PAAD), were retrieved. Among the 185 samples, 181 were tumor tissues, and 4 were normal tissues. After excluding 3 samples lacking survival information, the remaining samples were randomly assigned to the training and testing sets. Based on the selected immunogenic cell death-related lncRNAs, we constructed a prognostic model for pancreatic cancer. Additionally, we verified the effect of LINC00705 on pancreatic cancer cells PANC-1 and MiaPaca-2 through in vitro cell experiments. The results showed that LINC00705 significantly enhanced the proliferation, migration, and invasion abilities of pancreatic cancer cells PANC-1 and MiaPaca-2, providing a novel biomarker for the prediction of pancreatic cancer prognosis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-17T19:32:09.066Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v475rd4282","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v475rd4282"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hao","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Long Noncoding RNA"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2rrn8ngvsz","title":"Tumor-Associated Schwann Cell Remodeling under Metabolic Stress via Lactate Sensing Orchestrates","description":"Diabetes mellitus (DM) is a known risk factor for pancreatic cancer, but the underlying mechanisms remain elusive. Using in vivo models of diabetes-driven pancreatic tumorigenesis and metabolomics analysis, we demonstrate that metabolic stress induced by DM promotes pancreatic cancer progression by remodeling tumor-associated Schwann cells (TASs), impairing CD8+ T cell function. We discover that Mettl16+ Cd276+ Nectin2+ Schwann cells play a crucial role in mediating the pro-carcinogenic effects of lactate. Remodeled METTL16+ Schwann cells secret immune evasion factors (CD276, NECTIN2), contributing to anti-PD1 resistance. Furthermore, rosuvastatin was identified in this study as a novel METTL16 inhibitor for pancreatic cancer under metabolic stress. In conclusion, our study underscores the critical role of lactate in the metabolic reprogramming of pancreatic cancer mediated by Schwann cell remodeling, revealing novel pathways for therapeutic intervention.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-01T10:24:44.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2rrn8ngvsz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2rrn8ngvsz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baiyong","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tumor","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b3mydj4grf","title":"Kalli_RPPA_032021","description":"RPPA dataset for pancreatic cancer cells compressed","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-31T16:21:59.789Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b3mydj4grf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b3mydj4grf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ioannis","last_name":"Zervantonakis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"32ncbgtw59","title":"Tumor-Associated Schwann Cell Remodeling under Metabolic Stress via Lactate Sensing Orchestrates","description":"Diabetes mellitus (DM) is a known risk factor for pancreatic cancer, but the underlying mechanisms remain elusive. Using in vivo models of diabetes-driven pancreatic tumorigenesis and metabolomics analysis, we demonstrate that metabolic stress induced by DM promotes pancreatic cancer progression by remodeling tumor-associated Schwann cells (TASs), impairing CD8+ T cell function. We discover that Mettl16+ Cd276+ Nectin2+ Schwann cells play a crucial role in mediating the pro-carcinogenic effects of lactate. Remodeled METTL16+ Schwann cells secret immune evasion factors (CD276, NECTIN2), contributing to anti-PD1 resistance. Furthermore, rosuvastatin was identified in this study as a novel METTL16 inhibitor for pancreatic cancer under metabolic stress. In conclusion, our study underscores the critical role of lactate in the metabolic reprogramming of pancreatic cancer mediated by Schwann cell remodeling, revealing novel pathways for therapeutic intervention.\nBiotin pull-down assay\nThe Magic Dynabeads MyOne Streptavidin T1 were pre-incubated with free biotin or biotinylated lactate at room temperature for 30 minutes to saturate the bead surface with biotin. Subsequently, the pre-treated beads were mixed with the cell lysate of interest and incubated overnight at 4°C with rotation. The beads were then washed four times with PBS buffer to remove non-specifically bound molecules. After washing, the proteins were separated from the beads, and the presence and expression levels of the target protein were detected using techniques such as Western blotting or proteomics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-10T08:17:48.997Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/32ncbgtw59","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/32ncbgtw59"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baiyong","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Tumor","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p5yt2g3s7m","title":"DC-D-23-00339R2-Uncropped gel images with MW markers","description":"Uncropped gel images with MW markers of article \"The UBE2F-CRL5ASB11-DIRAS2 axis is an oncogene and tumor suppressor cascade in pancreatic cancer cells\" DC-D-23-00339R2","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-19T15:04:47.799Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p5yt2g3s7m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p5yt2g3s7m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx9xk9m6mm","title":"liver metastasis-associated myofibroblast-related genes ","description":"The expression of KRT18 in pancreatic cancer cell lines analyzed by qRT-PCR, and the colony formation, EdU,  Transwell, scratch wound formation assays in PaCa cells treated with overexpression  or repression of KRT 18 .","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-01T09:17:42.778Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx9xk9m6mm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx9xk9m6mm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jin","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer","Differential Gene Expression"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yfnkfrmhxn","title":"HNRNPC lactylation promotes pancreatic cancer progression through mediating the alternative splicing of PAK6","description":"This is a dataset that contains the RNAseq results of pancreatic cancer cells after the exhaustion of lactylation in HNRNPC.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-08T11:38:37.701Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yfnkfrmhxn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yfnkfrmhxn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"bohao","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":131763,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":306,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":131763,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":131223,"restricted":64,"embargoed":476},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2190},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agenci